{"name":"Sweet Pastel","hex":"#edc8b1","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sweet","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Tasting of sugars.","Retaining a portion of sugar."]},{"part":"Interjection","definitions":["Used as a positive response to good news or information."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sweet"},{"term":"pastel","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of several subdued tints of colors, usually associated with pink, peach, yellow, green, blue, and lavender.","A drawing made with any of those colors."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A traditional dish in various Latin American countries and in Indonesia, resembling a tamale, pasty, or calzone.","A Filipino stew made with vegetables, sausages, and chicken or other meat in a creamy sauce."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pastel"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sweetness","blocks":[{"text":"Sweetness is a basic taste most commonly perceived when eating foods rich in sugar. Sweet tastes are generally regarded as pleasurable. In addition to sugars like sucrose, many other chemical compounds are sweet, including aldehydes, ketones, and sugar alcohols. Some are sweet at very low concentrations, allowing their use as non-caloric sugar substitutes. Such non-sugar sweeteners include saccharin, aspartame, sucralose and stevia. Other compounds, such as miraculin, may alter perception of sweetness itself."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Strawberry_shortcake.jpg/330px-Strawberry_shortcake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sweetness"},{"title":"Pastel","blocks":[{"text":"A pastel is an art medium that consists of powdered pigment and a binder. It can exist in a variety of forms, including a stick, a square, a pebble, and a pan of color, among other forms. The pigments used in pastels are similar to those used to produce some other colored visual arts media, such as oil paints; the binder is of a neutral hue and low saturation. The color effect of pastels is closer to the natural dry pigments than that of any other process."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg/330px-William_Merritt_Chase%2C_Study_of_Flesh_Color_and_Gold%2C_1888%2C_NGA_103252.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastel"},{"title":"Pastel (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Pastels or pastel colors belong to a pale family of colors, which, when described in the HSV color space, have high value and low or medium saturation. They are named after the artistic medium made from pigment and solid binding agents, similar to crayons. Pastel sticks historically had lower saturation than paints of the same pigment, hence the name of this color family."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Commercial_pastels.JPG/330px-Commercial_pastels.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastel_(color)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/1280px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/40/Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg/500px-Boys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Boys Pilfering Molasses – On The Quays, New Orleans, 1853 painting by George Henry Hall","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABoys_Pilfering_Molasses_by_George_Henry_Hall.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5a/Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Gray1018.png/250px-Gray1018.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sweetness is perceived by the taste buds.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray1018.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp/250px-Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp/250px-Gut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Gut-brain reward pathway","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGut_Brain_Reward_Pathway.webp"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Overview_of_reward_structures_in_the_human_brain.jpg/500px-Overview_of_reward_structures_in_the_human_brain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Overview_of_reward_structures_in_the_human_brain.jpg/500px-Overview_of_reward_structures_in_the_human_brain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Reward structures in the human brain (dopamine / reward circuit)","link":"http